#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Derive this Codespace's identity into an env file, so the stack serves on the
# hostname the browser will actually use and admits the person who opened it.
#
# Usage:
#   scripts/codespace-env [--output PATH] [--force]
#
# Writes KEY=value lines. Default output is `.env.local`, which `scripts/dc`
# cascades on top of `.env` — that is the path for a developer driving the stack
# by hand. `docker/entrypoint.sh` calls it with `--output /run/tap-codespace.env`
# and sources the result, which is the path a Codespace actually takes; see
# WHERE THIS RUNS below for why there are two.
#
# It is a no-op outside a Codespace, and refuses to clobber an existing output
# file unless --force is given.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (gs#101)
# ------------------------
# A Codespace forwards its port on a hostname GENERATED PER CODESPACE —
# `<codespace-name>-<port>.app.github.dev`. Nothing in this repository can know
# it at build time, and four separate mechanisms need it or something derived
# from it:
#
#   1. ALLOWED_HOSTS          Django validates the Host header whether DEBUG is
#                             on or off. An unlisted host is a bare 400 — the
#                             correct failure and a terrible first run.
#   2. CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS   The browser's origin is https; the container serves
#                             http behind the forwarder. Every form POST fails
#                             without this, LOGIN INCLUDED — and it fails after
#                             the page has rendered perfectly, which reads as a
#                             broken credential rather than a missing setting.
#   3. SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER  Without it Django believes the request is
#                             plaintext, refuses to set a secure cookie, and
#                             request.is_secure() lies.
#   4. SECRET_KEY             There is no fallback any more (req-tap-serving-
#                             fail-closed): unset means the artifact refuses to
#                             start. Generated here, per Codespace, and PERSISTED
#                             (see below) because regenerating it on every boot
#                             would silently log everyone out on restart.
#
# one that is about WHO rather than WHERE:
#
#   5. TAP_AUTH_INSTANCE_OWNER  The account that opened this Codespace. A boot
#                             profile declaring `owner_only: true` states the
#                             INTENT; this supplies the VALUE, so no committed
#                             config ever hardcodes a person.
#
# and one that is about WHICH INSTANCE this is at all:
#
#   6. TAP_BOOT_PROFILE       Defaults to `codespace_demo`, the profile that
#                             declares the device-flow provider. Without it the
#                             entrypoint falls back to core_dev, which declares
#                             no auth provider — a correctly-served instance with
#                             nothing to sign in with. Override with
#                             TAP_CODESPACE_BOOT_PROFILE.
#
# WHERE THIS RUNS, AND WHY NOT ON THE HOST
# -----------------------------------------
# The obvious design — derive on the host before `compose up` — does not work
# under a compose-native devcontainer, and the reason is worth writing down
# because it is invisible until you try it.
#
# Compose performs `${VAR}` substitution from the shell environment and from
# `.env` in the project directory. It does NOT read `.env.local`; `scripts/dc`
# only makes that work by passing `--env-file` explicitly, and devcontainer.json
# has no hook to add that flag. `.env` is checked in, so a host-side step cannot
# write there either. A service-level `env_file:` would reach the container but
# loses to the `environment:` block, which is where these keys are declared.
#
# So the derivation runs INSIDE the container, from `docker/entrypoint.sh`,
# before Django reads settings. Codespaces injects its default variables into the
# dev container, so `CODESPACE_NAME` and friends are present there. The values
# become ordinary process environment, which is what settings.py reads — no
# compose substitution involved, and nothing to keep in step with a second file.
#
# The `.env.local` mode is kept for the human driving `scripts/dc` by hand.
#
# WHY THE OWNER IS DERIVED FROM THE USER, NOT THE REPOSITORY
# ----------------------------------------------------------
# `tap#741` considered deriving the owner from GITHUB_REPOSITORY and rejected it
# for a good reason: the repository is not the person. A visitor who opens a
# Codespace on OUR repo rather than their own copy would derive US as the owner
# and be locked out of the instance they just launched.
#
# GITHUB_USER does not have that failure. GitHub defines it as "the name of the
# user that initiated the codespace" and GITHUB_TOKEN as "a signed auth token
# representing the user in the codespace" — neither is a function of which
# repository the Codespace was opened on. So this derivation is correct under
# both launch paths, which is the property the rejected one lacked.
#
# The id must be NUMERIC. `instance_owner()` authorizes on the numeric user id
# alone and treats a login as unresolvable, because a handle can be renamed by
# its holder and re-registered by someone else — the defect `PR# 688 - tap`
# fixed. GITHUB_USER is a handle, so it is resolved to an id here and the handle
# is carried only for diagnostics.

set -euo pipefail

# The repo root, resolved from this script's own location rather than from git.
# The container has no guarantee of a usable git checkout at boot, and a
# derivation that depends on one fails at exactly the moment it is needed.
cd "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)"

output=".env.local"
force=false
while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
  case "$1" in
    --output) output="${2:?--output needs a path}"; shift 2 ;;
    --force)  force=true; shift ;;
    *) echo "unknown argument: $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done

if [[ "${CODESPACES:-}" != "true" ]]; then
  echo "Not running in a Codespace (CODESPACES is not 'true') — nothing to derive." >&2
  exit 0
fi

: "${CODESPACE_NAME:?CODESPACE_NAME is unset inside a Codespace — cannot derive the forwarded hostname}"
: "${GITHUB_CODESPACES_PORT_FORWARDING_DOMAIN:?unset inside a Codespace — cannot derive the forwarded hostname}"

if [[ -e "$output" && "$force" != true ]]; then
  echo "refusing to overwrite an existing ${output} (pass --force to replace it)" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# The port the forwarder publishes. Inside the container the app listens on 8000
# regardless of WEB_PORT, which maps a HOST port; the Codespace forwards the host
# port, so WEB_PORT is the right input and 8000 is the right fallback.
web_port="${WEB_PORT:-8000}"
forwarded_host="${CODESPACE_NAME}-${web_port}.${GITHUB_CODESPACES_PORT_FORWARDING_DOMAIN}"
forwarded_origin="https://${forwarded_host}"

# --- who opened this Codespace -------------------------------------------------
#
# Two routes to the numeric id, in order of preference:
#
#   GET /user            authenticated as the visitor. Exact by construction — it
#                        asks "who am I?" rather than "who holds this handle?" —
#                        but it depends on the injected token carrying user scope,
#                        which is not guaranteed.
#   GET /users/<login>   public, unauthenticated, always available. Resolves the
#                        handle GitHub just told us belongs to this session. The
#                        theoretical gap is a rename between Codespace creation
#                        and this lookup, a window of seconds.
#
# A failure to resolve is NOT fatal. An unresolved owner leaves
# TAP_AUTH_INSTANCE_OWNER empty, and a provider declaring `owner_only` against an
# empty owner DENIES every login (`policy_unresolvable`) and FAILs its offline
# self-test. That is the designed fail-closed direction: this script must never
# turn "I could not tell who you are" into "everyone may log in".
api="${GITHUB_API_URL:-https://api.github.com}"
owner_login="${GITHUB_USER:-}"
owner_id=""

_extract_id() { sed -n 's/.*"id"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*\([0-9][0-9]*\).*/\1/p' | head -1; }

if [[ -n "${GITHUB_TOKEN:-}" ]]; then
  owner_id="$(curl -fsS -H "Authorization: Bearer ${GITHUB_TOKEN}" \
                   -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
                   "${api}/user" 2>/dev/null | _extract_id || true)"
fi

if [[ -z "$owner_id" && -n "$owner_login" ]]; then
  owner_id="$(curl -fsS -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
                   "${api}/users/${owner_login}" 2>/dev/null | _extract_id || true)"
fi

# The login is echoed into JSON, so it must not be able to break out of the
# string. GitHub logins are [A-Za-z0-9-] and nothing else; anything that is not
# is dropped rather than escaped — deriving a safe value beats quoting a
# dangerous one.
if [[ ! "$owner_login" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9-]+$ ]]; then
  owner_login=""
fi

owner_json=""
if [[ -n "$owner_id" ]]; then
  owner_json="{\"login\": \"${owner_login}\", \"user_id\": \"${owner_id}\"}"
  echo "==> Codespace owner resolved: ${owner_login:-<unknown login>} (id=${owner_id})" >&2
else
  echo "==> WARN: could not resolve a numeric GitHub user id." >&2
  echo "==>       TAP_AUTH_INSTANCE_OWNER stays empty, so a provider declaring" >&2
  echo "==>       owner_only will DENY every login rather than admit anyone." >&2
fi

# --- a signing key that survives a restart --------------------------------------
#
# Generated rather than shipped: SECRET_KEY has no fallback and the development
# value is refused by digest at boot. PERSISTED under the workspace, which is the
# Codespace's own durable storage, because a key regenerated on every container
# start invalidates every session cookie — the visitor would be logged out by a
# restart with no explanation. 0600, and gitignored.
key_file=".codespace-secret-key"
if [[ ! -s "$key_file" ]]; then
  ( umask 077; python3 -c 'import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(64))' > "$key_file" )
fi
secret_key="$(cat "$key_file")"

umask 077
cat > "$output" <<ENVFILE
# GENERATED by scripts/codespace-env — do not commit, do not hand-edit.
# Regenerate with: scripts/codespace-env --output ${output} --force
#
# Codespace: ${CODESPACE_NAME}
# Forwarded origin: ${forwarded_origin}

# The Host header this instance answers to. Django validates it regardless of DEBUG.
ALLOWED_HOSTS=${forwarded_host},localhost,127.0.0.1,.localhost

# The origins the browser posts FROM. Scheme-qualified, and https where the
# container serves http — that mismatch is the whole reason this line exists.
#
# THREE origins, not one, because a Codespace is reachable by two different names and
# ALLOWED_HOSTS above already admits both. The line used to carry only the forwarded
# origin, and the result was a login that failed for a visitor doing nothing unusual:
#
#     Forbidden (Origin checking failed - http://localhost:8000
#                does not match any trusted origins.): /auth/device/start/
#
# OBSERVED 2026-09-22 on codespace super-happiness-g7p5r666729r7j.
#
# THE CAUSE: GitHub's `*.app.github.dev` WEB PROXY terminates the public request and presents
# it to the container as `Origin: http://localhost:<port>` — the origin it proxies INTO. The
# browser was on the public HTTPS URL the whole time. So ANY app served through the Codespaces
# web proxy that performs an origin check must trust the localhost origin; this is not
# TAP-specific and is worth knowing before debugging it from scratch somewhere else.
#
# GETs have no origin check, so the page rendered perfectly and only the POST failed — which
# is why this reads as a broken button rather than a configuration gap.
#
# A CORRECTION, LEFT IN because the wrong version of this comment shipped for two commits and
# someone will otherwise re-run the same experiment and reach the same wrong answer. The first
# explanation here said VS Code's port forwarding had put the browser on localhost, and cited
# as proof that a POST carrying `https://MARKER-probe.example.invalid` through
# `gh codespace ports forward` reached Django with the marker intact.
#
# That experiment was real, and it proves nothing about this. There are TWO DIFFERENT
# FORWARDING PATHS into a Codespace:
#
#   * `gh codespace ports forward` — the CLI tunnel. PRESERVES the browser's Origin.
#   * `https://<name>-<port>.app.github.dev` — the web proxy. REWRITES it to localhost.
#
# The marker went through the tunnel; the user went through the proxy. Testing one path and
# concluding about the other is the trap, and the two paths disagreeing is precisely what
# makes it a trap worth writing down.
#
# Scope: these are localhost origins INSIDE a per-user Codespace, admitted by a file that
# is only ever written inside one, on an instance whose ALLOWED_HOSTS already answers to
# localhost. This does not widen a deployment — nothing outside this Codespace can present
# them.
CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=${forwarded_origin},http://localhost:${web_port},http://127.0.0.1:${web_port}

# Believe the forwarder's protocol header, so secure cookies are set and
# request.is_secure() tells the truth behind TLS termination.
TAP_SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER=HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO,https

# This instance's public identity, used to derive provider callback URLs.
TAP_BASE_URL=${forwarded_origin}

# Per-Codespace signing key, persisted so a restart does not log everyone out.
SECRET_KEY=${secret_key}

# The account that opened this Codespace. Empty means unresolved, which DENIES.
TAP_AUTH_INSTANCE_OWNER=${owner_json}

# The role that owner gets on first sign-in, keyed on the SAME (provider, uid) the line above
# authorizes with — never on email, which nothing can know in advance here (the Codespace token
# is repository-scoped; codespace_demo's own description says so).
#
# Without this the Codespace admits exactly one person and then hands them
# `Forbidden (capability_denied)` on a fully successful sign-in — an instance that lets you in
# the door and gives you no room to enter. OBSERVED 2026-09-22 on a live Codespace, after every
# other value on this page had derived correctly.
#
# Empty everywhere else: settings.TAP_AUTH_OWNER_ROLE defaults to "" and this file is only ever
# written inside a Codespace, so no other deployment gains an owner grant by existing.
TAP_AUTH_OWNER_ROLE=${TAP_CODESPACE_OWNER_ROLE:-tap_admin}

# The profile this instance boots. Without this the entrypoint falls back to
# core_dev, which declares no auth provider at all — the instance would serve
# correctly on its derived hostname and offer nothing to sign in with, which is
# the confusing half-success this line exists to avoid.
TAP_BOOT_PROFILE=${TAP_CODESPACE_BOOT_PROFILE:-codespace_demo}

# Where the login wall sends an anonymous visitor.
#
# OBSERVED 2026-09-22 on codespace super-happiness-g7p5r666729r7j, which had every
# line above derived correctly and STILL showed a passkey prompt: settings.LOGIN_URL
# is the module-level constant "passkey_login", so TapLoginRequiredMiddleware
# redirects there no matter what the boot profile configured. The visitor then met a
# WebAuthn ceremony pinned to a canonical origin that is not this Codespace, plus an
# offer to "sign in at the canonical address instead" pointing at localhost:8000.
#
# That is a KNOWN gap, not a regression — tap/settings.py's own comment says full
# repointing is Phase 3 (spec-tap-auth-passkey-v0 webauthn-6/slim-6). This line does
# not implement Phase 3. It repoints the login wall for CODESPACES ONLY, where the
# answer is unambiguous because codespace_demo declares exactly one provider, disables
# local passwords, and cannot do passkeys at all on a per-instance hostname.
#
# An ordinary install is untouched: settings falls back to "passkey_login" when this
# key is absent, and this file is only ever written inside a Codespace.
TAP_LOGIN_URL=${TAP_CODESPACE_LOGIN_URL:-device_login}
ENVFILE

echo "==> wrote ${output} for ${forwarded_origin}" >&2
